          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong finite limit
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            over a finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
            \- that is, one whose shape is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+category"} finite category
          %p
            More generally, in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            of a diagram that is a finite
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2Cr%29-category"} (n,r)-category
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            that has all finite limits is called a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+category"} finitely complete category
            or a (finitary)
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+algebraic+theory"} essentially algebraic theory
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preserved+limit"} preserves
            all finite limits is called
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+exact+functor"} left exact functor
            = succeed "," do
              %em lex functor
            = succeed "," do
              %em cartesian functor
            = succeed "." do
              %em finitely continuous functor
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+categories"} finitely complete categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+exact+functors"} left exact functors
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
            is often denoted
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lex"} Lex
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
              is a finite limit iff it is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+product"} finite product
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indexed+set"} indexed
              by a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
            %p
              In particular,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+products"} binary products
              are finite limits and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
              (being the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
              over the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+diagram"} empty diagram
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              Other common finite limits are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullbacks"} pullbacks
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizers"} equalizers
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+locus"} fixed locus
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+action"} group action
              is a finite limit if the group in question is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+group"} finite group
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
              into
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+generated+topological+spaces"} compactly generated topological spaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preserved+limit"} preserves
              finite limits (is a
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+exact+functor"} left exact functor
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "geometric+realization#GeometricRealizationIsLeftExact"} there
            %p
              More generally, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization+of+simplicial+topological+spaces"} geometric realization of simplicial topological spaces
              (with respect to
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+generated+topological+spaces"} compactly generated topological spaces
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "geometric+realization+of+simplicial+topological+spaces#GeometricRealizationPreservesFiniteLimits"} this Prop.
          %h2#Properties Properties
          #FiniteLimitsFromPullbacksAndTerminalObject.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              For a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              the following are equivalent:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                  has all
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                  has all
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizers"} equalizers
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+products"} binary products
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                  has all
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullbacks"} pullbacks
                  and a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            %p
              In fact, either class of limits may be expressed in terms of another, so that for a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \;\colon\; \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{D}
              the following are equivalent:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preserved+limit"} preserves
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preserved+limit"} preserves
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizers"} equalizers
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+products"} binary products
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preserved+limit"} preserves
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullbacks"} pullbacks
                  and the
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
          %p
            (The first statement may be found, e.g., in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Borceux94"} Borceux 1994, Prop. 2.8.2
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              The equivalence of the first two items in Prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FiniteLimitsFromPullbacksAndTerminalObject"}
              is the finite analog of how a category with equalizers and all small products has all
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+limits"} small limits
              %br/
          %p
          #LFiniteLimits.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/saturation+of+a+class+of+limits"} saturation
                to
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-finite+limits"} L-finite limits
              %br/
              Prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FiniteLimitsFromPullbacksAndTerminalObject"}
              implies that finite limits are contained in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/saturation+of+a+class+of+limits"} saturation
              of the class containing only
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+products"} finite products
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizers"} equalizers
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullbacks"} pullbacks
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+objects"} terminal objects
            %p
              But the existence of finite limits in fact implies that of the larger class of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-finite+limits"} L-finite limits
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#Pare90"} Paré 1990, Prop 7
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "L-finite+category#CharacterizationsOfLFiniteLimits"} there
            %p
              Therefore Prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FiniteLimitsFromPullbacksAndTerminalObject"}
              implies that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-finite+limits"} L-finite limits
              constitute also the full
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/saturation+of+a+class+of+limits"} saturation class
              of both equalizers+finite products as well as pullbacks+terminal objects.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
